      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2347.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 618,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the most abundant and essential substance for life on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of water in all its fascinating aspects:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Unique States of Water\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** What we drink, swim in, and what fills our oceans and rivers.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes. (Uniquely, solid water is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats).\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, clouds, and steam from boiling water.\n\n### 2. Why Water is Essential for Life\nNo known form of life can survive without water. It plays crucial roles in biology:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Water dissolves more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a transportation system, carrying vital nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through the bodies of plants and animals (and through human blood).\n*   **Chemical Reactions:** Water is the medium in which most cellular processes and metabolic reactions take place.\n*   **Temperature Regulation:** Water has a high \"heat capacity,\" meaning it can absorb and release large amounts of heat. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and keeps living organisms from overheating or freezing.\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans and seas.\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite how much water exists on Earth, about **96.5% of it is salt water** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is fresh water**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 4. Fascinating Physical Properties\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n*   **Neutral pH:** Pure water has a pH level of 7, meaning it is neither acidic nor basic.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is the lifeblood of our planet. It shapes our geography, regulates our climate, makes up roughly 60% of the human body, and is the fundamental requirement for the existence of life as we know it."
